      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "route_only"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The hamlet’s name dates to the 1800s, a quirky choice that has made it a favorite spot for trivia lovers.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/surprise_new_york",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "A combined post office and general store built around 1910 served as the town’s social hub until the Blenis family closed it in 1985.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6,
        29
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/surprise_new_york",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Surprise’s early economy relied on oxen‑drawn hay harvesting, a practice that lingered into the early 20th century on farms still owned by families tracing back to 1791 settlers.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/surprise_new_york",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The surrounding landscape blends gently sloping Hudson River plains with rolling Catskill hills, creating fertile valleys that attracted settlers in the late 1700s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19,
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/surprise_new_york",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Surprise’s post office opened on February 25, 1889, and remained a vital community hub until its closure on October 8, 1988.",
